The php script is vulnerable to a blind SQL injection attack.  It passes the $_REQUEST['username']
directly into the SQL statement.  Unlike in natas16 where you can get a text string that says
whether a user exists or not, there is no output that the script passes back.  However, since
MySQL supports a SLEEP() function and since conjunctive AND statements are short-cut as soon
as a false expression is reached, you can construct an iterative search using REGEXPs on the
password.  If the sleep after the regexp is hit, then the regexp matched the password and
you will get a large delay in the web page that is returned.  If it is not hit, you get the
web page back immediately.  The python script encodes a timeout to test this condition and to
use it in a binary search on the regexp until the next character of the password is ascertained.

